    "result_text": "The full suite is green (10 tests, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI extended the business-day rule for the registration date to skip listed public holidays, placing the change where it belongs \u2014 in the `BusinessDay` calendar helper, which is the single authority for what counts as a business day.\n\n**`util/BusinessDay.java`** \u2014 added the fixed public-holiday set and folded it into `isBusinessDay`:\n- New `PUBLIC_HOLIDAYS` set: `2026-01-01, 2026-01-26, 2026-04-25, 2026-12-25, 2026-12-28`.\n- New `isPublicHoliday(date)` predicate.\n- `isBusinessDay` now returns false for weekends **and** public holidays.\n- `onOrAfter` needed no logic change: its existing roll-forward loop now automatically skips holidays too (e.g. Fri 2026-12-25 \u2192 weekend \u2192 Mon 2026-12-28, also a holiday \u2192 Tue 2026-12-29).\n\nBecause every downstream step (`ResolveRegistrationDate`, daily-limit, bulk-signup, membership number, tenure) already keys off the adjusted date via `onOrAfter`, no other production code needed touching. I updated the `ResolveRegistrationDate` javadoc to reflect the extended rule.\n\n**`test/util/BusinessDayTest.java`** (new) \u2014 9 unit tests covering weekdays, weekends, each holiday's roll-forward, and the chained Christmas/28th case. The existing black-box `AcceptanceTest` continues to pass.\n\nNo single class became a catch-all \u2014 the holiday calendar lives with the rest of the calendar rules, and each step keeps its single responsibility.",
